---
// An /explain artifact: when an agent loop should become a graph. Adapted from my own
// prototype (Protozoa 1025997), public-safe: no proprietary detail.
//
// FOLDED INTO THE DOCUMENT (chooser: andrewshiau-grid-options, Q5/02) — see canele.astro for
// what that means and what it replaced. This page keeps one bespoke device, the flow diagram,
// rebuilt on the system at the bottom of this file.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const quiz = [
  { q: "What actually distinguishes a graph from a loop?",
    opts:[
      "Graphs are non-linear and loops are strictly linear.",
      "Loops are the old, deprecated approach; graphs are the modern replacement.",
      "Work is split into fixed nodes joined by edges, and some edges are gates the flow must pass through.",
      "Graphs use multiple agents; loops use exactly one.",
    ], a:2 },
  { q: "A team wraps a single task — write a function, run tests, fix, repeat until green — into a 4-node graph. Tests still self-verify. What did they gain?",
    opts:[
      "Typed state, which stops the agent from hallucinating.",
      "Higher reliability, because graph-structured agents are inherently more trustworthy.",
      "Essentially nothing — it collapses back into one loop, so they only bought latency and rigidity.",
      "Separation of task and evaluation.",
    ], a:2 },
  { q: "Which is a legitimate reason to escalate a loop into a graph?",
    opts:[
      "A graph is easier to draw and looks more professional in a design review.",
      "You want the agent to iterate a few more times before stopping.",
      "A step must be blocked until a human approves it before the flow may continue.",
      "The loop’s system prompt is getting long and unwieldy.",
    ], a:2 },
  { q: "The critique \"the same agent that did the task grades the task\" points at which property of a loop?",
    opts:[
      "Loops keep no memory between iterations.",
      "Loops can’t call external tools.",
      "Self-verification — the actor is also the judge, so it can optimize its own metric past the point the metric still means anything.",
      "Loops run forever and never terminate.",
    ], a:2 },
  { q: "How should \"the shift from loops to graphs\" best be understood?",
    opts:[
      "As a rule that any multi-step agent must be a graph.",
      "As an industry consensus that loop-based agents are now deprecated.",
      "As a benchmark result proving graphs complete tasks faster.",
      "As a question about whether your setup needs gates yet — not a settled migration everyone must perform.",
    ], a:3 },
  { q: "Pipeline: research → draft → fact-check → publish, where fact-check must block publish and a human signs off before publish. Loop or graph?",
    opts:[
      "Loop — graphs are only for parallel or branching work.",
      "Graph — there’s a hard gate (human sign-off blocking an edge) plus distinct stages a single self-judging loop can’t enforce.",
      "Loop — one agent could just perform all four steps in sequence.",
      "Graph — because four steps is simply too many for a loop to handle.",
    ], a:1 },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Loops vs graphs — Andrew Shiau"
  description="An interactive explainer on agent architecture: when a loop should become a graph, and when it shouldn’t."
  quiz={quiz}
>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="pin">
      <p class="smark">Explainer</p>
      <h1>Loops vs graphs</h1>
      <div class="facts">
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">What</div>
          <div class="ftx">Agent architecture: when a loop should become a graph, and when it
            shouldn’t. <b>A test you can run on any agent you build.</b></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">Prompted by</div>
          <div class="ftx">Peter Steinberger’s July 2026 “loops or graphs?” question. Adapted
            from my own prototype on agent architecture; public-safe, no proprietary
            detail.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">Back to</div>
          <div class="ftx"><a href="/work/explain/">Explain</a></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="splitrule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="feed">
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">In July 2026 Peter Steinberger poked the field with one line — “are
          we still talking loops, or did we shift to graphs yet?” It read as a verdict. It
          wasn’t. It was a question, and the answer is a test you can run on any agent you
          build.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="idea c1-12">
          <span class="k">The one idea</span>
          <p>A loop is one agent iterating on its own judgment until it’s done. A graph cuts that
            work into fixed nodes joined by gated edges. Escalate only to buy a gate,
            determinism, or a real handoff.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2><span class="sn">1</span>The two shapes</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>These are not “old vs new.” A loop is a cycle; a graph is a fixed path with
              checkpoints. The defining difference isn’t how many agents there are — it’s <b>who decides what happens next</b>: the model’s own judgment (loop), or a
              structure you wired in advance (graph).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="flow c1-6">
          <span class="fh">Loop — one agent, iterating</span>
          <div class="node"><span class="nl">Step</span><span class="nt">Observe</span><span class="nd">Read the goal and current state.</span></div>
          <div class="arr">↓</div>
          <div class="node"><span class="nl">Step</span><span class="nt">Act</span><span class="nd">Pick one tool, run it, see the result.</span></div>
          <div class="arr">↓</div>
          <div class="node crux"><span class="nl">The crux</span><span class="nt">Self-check</span><span class="nd">The same agent judges “done?” — actor and judge are one.</span></div>
          <div class="loopback">↺ not done → back to Observe</div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow c7-12">
          <span class="fh">Graph — fixed nodes, gated edges</span>
          <div class="node"><span class="nl">Node A</span><span class="nt">Do stage one</span><span class="nd">Typed state passes along the edge.</span></div>
          <div class="arr">↓</div>
          <div class="node gate"><span class="nl">The edge that earns it</span><span class="nt">Gate</span><span class="nd">A hard check or human approval the flow cannot pass without.</span></div>
          <div class="arr">↓</div>
          <div class="node"><span class="nl">Node B</span><span class="nt">Do stage two</span><span class="nd">Only reachable once the gate cleared.</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="block c1-12">
          <p>The crux node is the loop’s problem — it grades its own work. The gate is the only
            thing a graph gives you that a loop can’t: an edge the flow is forced through.
            Everything else a graph “adds” — more steps, more prompts — a loop already has.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2><span class="sn">2</span>When a graph earns its place</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>There are exactly three honest reasons to break a loop into a graph. If none
              apply, you’re adding latency and rigidity for tidiness.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="frow"><div class="fm">Gate</div><div class="ftx">A step must be blocked until a hard condition passes or a human approves. A self-judging loop can’t enforce a stop it isn’t allowed to overrule.</div></div>
          <div class="frow"><div class="fm">Determinism</div><div class="ftx">You need a boundary to behave the same way every time — a fixed route, a checkpoint you can resume from — not “whatever the model decided this run.”</div></div>
          <div class="frow"><div class="fm">Real handoff</div><div class="ftx">Stages genuinely differ — different tools or models per node (cheap researcher → strong writer → strict reviewer), not one agent play-acting all three.</div></div>
          <div class="frow"><div class="fm">Not a reason</div><div class="ftx">“The prompt got long.” “Four steps feels like a lot.” “A diagram looks more professional.” Step count and neatness aren’t reasons.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="idea c1-12">
          <span class="k">The collapse test</span>
          <p>Try to fold every node back into one agent’s loop. If you can and lose nothing — no
            gate goes unenforced, no determinism disappears, no real tool/model split collapses —
            it was never a graph. It was a loop wearing a diagram, and you paid latency for the
            costume.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="block c1-12">
          <p>Rule of thumb: if five nodes collapse back into one loop and nothing is lost, you
            should collapse them. Master the loop first. Reach for a graph only when the work
            forces your hand.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2><span class="sn">3</span>Check yourself</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>Six questions. The answer appears when you pick one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid"><div class="quiz c1-12" id="quiz"></div></div>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="foot c1-12">
          <a href="/work/explain/">Explain</a> ·
          <a href="/">andrewshiau.com</a>
          <span class="kb"> · Press <code>⌥G</code> for the grid</span>
          <span class="tou"> · Set on a 12-column grid</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  /* THE FLOW DIAGRAM — this page's one bespoke device, rebuilt on the system.
     What came out: 2px ink rules on every node, weight-700 17px titles, uppercase tracked
     10px labels, and TWO FILLED PANELS — one inverted to ink, one filled with the accent. The
     filled accent node was the worst of it: colour means "you can act on this" everywhere else
     on the site, and that was a static box.
     What replaces them is the ledger's own vocabulary, because a node IS a label/value pair:
     hairline above, faint label, ink title, dim description. The two nodes that carry meaning
     are distinguished the way the marker is — by the one thing that survives greyscale, which
     here is the label text itself ("The crux", "The edge that earns it") plus `--ink` on the
     title. The ⛔ emoji is gone with the fill; the word "Gate" is the label. */
  .flow{align-self:start}
  .flow .fh{display:block;color:var(--faint);margin-bottom:var(--tight);line-height:1.2}
  .flow .node{border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:var(--tight);
    margin-bottom:var(--tight)}
  .flow .node .nl{display:block;color:var(--faint);line-height:1.2}
  .flow .node .nt{display:block;color:var(--ink)}
  .flow .node .nd{display:block;color:var(--dim)}
  /* The two nodes that are the argument. A heavier hairline, not a fill: the line is the node's
     own top edge (it touches its content), which is the one place global.css still allows a
     rule — see THE ONE HAIRLINE. `<b>`-weight on the title is the site's one emphasis device. */
  .flow .node.crux,.flow .node.gate{border-top-color:var(--ink)}
  .flow .node.crux .nt,.flow .node.gate .nt{font-weight:500}
  .flow .arr{color:var(--faint);padding:0 0 var(--tight)}
  .flow .loopback{color:var(--faint)}
  @media(max-width:1060px){
    .flow{margin-top:var(--group)}
    .flow:first-of-type{margin-top:0}
  }
</style>
